  <title>Adversarial Robustness of Explainable AI: Instability, Metrics, and Deployment Risks</title>
  
<style> @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Barlow+Semi+Condensed:wght@600;700&family=Barlow:wght@400;500;600;700&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap'); :root { --nx-bg: #000000; --nx-bg-elevated: #0a0a0a; --nx-bg-panel: #111111; --nx-text-hero: #f0f0fa; --nx-text-primary: #f0f0fa; --nx-text-secondary: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.62); --nx-text-tertiary: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.35); --nx-text-quaternary: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.25); --nx-border-subtle: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.08); --nx-border-medium: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.14); --nx-border-cta: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.35); --nx-read-color: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.78); --nx-radius: 4px; --font-sans: 'Barlow', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif; --font-display: 'Barlow Semi Condensed', 'Arial Narrow', sans-serif; --font-mono: 'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace; } * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; } body { background: var(--nx-bg); color: var(--nx-text-primary); font-family: var(--font-sans); -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; -moz-osx-font-smoothing: grayscale; } article.nomosx-brief { max-width: 42rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 2rem 5rem; font-family: var(--font-sans); color: var(--nx-read-color); font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.75; background: var(--nx-bg); position: relative; } /* NomosX grid background */ article.nomosx-brief::before { content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.025) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.025) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 72px 72px; -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 60% at 50% 30%, black 0%, transparent 70%); mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 60% at 50% 30%, black 0%, transparent 70%); z-index: 0; } article.nomosx-brief > * { position: relative; z-index: 1; } /* Brand header */ .nx-brand-header { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 3rem; padding-bottom: 2rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); } .nx-brand-mark { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--nx-text-hero); letter-spacing: -0.01em; text-transform: uppercase; } .nx-brand-mark .x-glyph { color: var(--nx-text-hero); } .nx-brand-tag { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.5625rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.18em; color: var(--nx-text-tertiary); } h1 { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); font-weight: 700; line-height: 0.92; letter-spacing: -0.01em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--nx-text-hero); margin-bottom: 1rem; } .brief-meta { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; color: var(--nx-text-tertiary); margin-bottom: 3rem; padding-bottom: 1.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); letter-spacing: 0.04em; } .brief-section { margin: 2.5rem 0; } .brief-section h2 { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--nx-text-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem; padding-bottom: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; } .brief-section h3 { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--nx-text-secondary); margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; } .brief-section p { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; color: var(--nx-read-color); } .brief-section strong { color: var(--nx-text-primary); font-weight: 600; } .brief-section a { color: var(--nx-text-primary); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-border-medium); } .prose-block { margin: 1.5rem 0; } .prose-block p { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; } .through-line { font-size: 1.1em; font-weight: 500; color: var(--nx-text-secondary); margin: 1.5em 0; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--nx-border-medium); } .debate-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.25rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; } .debate-pro, .debate-con { background: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.02); border: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); border-radius: var(--nx-radius); padding: 1.25rem; } .debate-pro { border-top: 2px solid var(--nx-text-secondary); } .debate-con { border-top: 2px solid var(--nx-text-quaternary); } .debate-pro h3, .debate-con h3 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.625rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--nx-text-tertiary); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; } .debate-synthesis { background: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.02); border: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); border-top: 2px solid var(--nx-text-primary); border-radius: var(--nx-radius); padding: 1.25rem; margin-top: 1rem; } .debate-synthesis h3 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.625rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--nx-text-secondary); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; } .key-findings-card { background: rgba(240, 240, 250, 0.015); border: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); border-left: 2px solid var(--nx-text-primary); border-radius: var(--nx-radius); padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; margin: 1.75rem 0; } .key-findings-card h3 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.625rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--nx-text-tertiary); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; } .key-findings-card ul { padding-left: 1.25rem; } .key-findings-card li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--nx-read-color); } sup.cite a, sup.citation { color: var(--nx-text-secondary); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.75em; font-weight: 600; font-family: var(--font-mono); padding: 0 1px; vertical-align: super; } sup.cite a:hover { color: var(--nx-text-primary); } .references-section { margin-top: 4rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); } .references-section h2 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.625rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--nx-text-tertiary); margin-bottom: 1.25rem; padding-bottom: 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); } .apa-ref { display: block; padding: 0.5rem 0 0.5rem 2rem; text-indent: -2rem; font-size: 0.8125rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--nx-text-secondary); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); } .apa-ref:last-child { border-bottom: none; } .ref-num { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 500; color: var(--nx-text-tertiary); min-width: 1.75rem; display: inline-block; } .apa-ref em { color: var(--nx-text-primary); } /* Hero image */ .hero-image { margin: 0 0 2rem 0; width: 100%; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--nx-border-subtle); border-radius: var(--nx-radius); } .hero-image img { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; object-fit: cover; } @media print { article.nomosx-brief { padding: 1.5rem; max-width: 100%; } .debate-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } article.nomosx-brief::before { display: none; } } @media (max-width: 640px) { article.nomosx-brief { padding: 1.5rem 1rem; } h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; } .debate-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
</head>
<body>
  <article class="nomosx-brief">
    <div class="nx-brand-header">
  <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" aria-label="NomosX" role="img" style="flex-shrink:0;">
    <line x1="7" y1="7" x2="25" y2="25" stroke="#f0f0fa" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    <line x1="25" y1="7" x2="7" y2="25" stroke="#f0f0fa" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    <circle cx="16" cy="16" r="2.6" fill="#f0f0fa" stroke="none"/>
  </svg>
  <span class="nx-brand-mark">NOMOS<span class="x-glyph">X</span></span>
  <span class="nx-brand-tag">From Chaos to Nomos</span>
</div>
    <h1>Adversarial Robustness of Explainable AI: Instability, Metrics, and Deployment Risks</h1>
    <div class="brief-meta">August 20, 2026 · 56 sources</div>
<figure class="hero-image" style="margin:0 0 32px 0;width:100%;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid rgba(240,240,250,0.08);border-radius:4px;">
        <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64," alt="Adversarial Robustness of Explainable AI: Instability, Metrics, and Deployment Risks" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;object-fit:cover;" />
      </figure>

    <div class="prose-block"><p>The central finding is that post-hoc explainability methods, particularly SHAP and LIME, are demonstrably unstable under adversarial perturbations, a vulnerability that persists across medical imaging, text classification, and cybersecurity domains  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-16" data-evidence="E16">16</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-18" data-evidence="E18">18</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-39" data-evidence="E39">39</a></sup>. This instability is not a peripheral concern but a structural weakness: adversarial perturbations can alter explanations without changing model predictions, creating a false sense of trust in high-stakes decisions  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. The evidence indicates that current XAI evaluation practices are inadequate, relying on anecdotal evidence or expert opinion rather than robust metrics  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7" data-evidence="E7">7</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. This gap is compounded by the absence of standardized benchmarks, such as the Feature Attribution Stability Suite (FASS), which enforces prediction invariance as a precondition for stability measurement  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. The trajectory is clear: as XAI adoption grows in regulated sectors like healthcare and finance, the lack of robustness standards will become a critical failure point, potentially triggering regulatory intervention and driving demand for standardized evaluation frameworks  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup>. However, the causal chain is probabilistic, not deterministic <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4" data-evidence="E4">4</a></sup>. The impact depends on the rate of XAI adoption, the development of robust metrics like RoSHAP  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-36" data-evidence="E36">36</a></sup>, and the willingness of regulators to mandate standards. If these factors align, the field mature; if not, the risks will materialize as misdiagnoses, unfair recourse, and eroded trust <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. The confidence in this projection is tempered by the heterogeneity of XAI methods and the limited scope of existing studies, which often focus on specific domains like medical imaging  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-25" data-evidence="E25">25</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup> or remote sensing <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-13" data-evidence="E13">13</a></sup>. Nonetheless, the convergence of evidence across multiple domains and source types—academic, technical, and applied—strengthens the core claim: XAI robustness is a pressing, unresolved problem <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>.</p></div>
<p>Post-hoc XAI methods remain unstable under adversarial perturbations, and without standardized robustness metrics, their deployment in high-stakes domains poses significant risks <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="prose-block"><p>The analysis is grounded in a systematic evaluation of XAI robustness across multiple domains <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup>. A 2025 study on medical image recognition found that small perturbations to input can lead to substantial changes in explanations even when model prediction does not change  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. This finding is corroborated by a 2026 study on SMS spam detection, which showed that adversarial perturbations alter feature importance, diminishing the influence of spam-indicative tokens  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-18" data-evidence="E18">18</a></sup>. In the financial domain, SHAP produced more stable and comprehensive explanations than LIME, but both were vulnerable to instability under perturbations  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-16" data-evidence="E16">16</a></sup>. The lack of standardized evaluation metrics is a recurring theme: a systematic literature review of XAI applications found that most evaluations rely on anecdotal evidence or expert opinion rather than robust metrics  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>. This gap is further highlighted by the introduction of novel benchmarks like FASS, which enforces prediction invariance as a precondition for stability measurement  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>, and RoSHAP, a robust metric for stable feature attribution <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-36" data-evidence="E36">36</a></sup>. These sources, spanning academic journals, conference proceedings, and preprints, provide a convergent evidence base for the core claim of XAI instability [E36][E37].</p></div>
<div class="prose-block"><p>Post-hoc XAI methods, including SHAP and LIME, are widely used in high-stakes domains such as healthcare, finance, and law  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-23" data-evidence="E23">23</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup>. A systematic literature review found that XAI applications are predominantly in health-related domains, with a strong focus on cancer diagnosis, COVID-19 management, and medical imaging  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>. Explainability is a key criterion for reinforcing confidence in AI-based medical devices  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup>. The mechanism: The widespread adoption of XAI in critical domains creates a demand for reliable explanations, which are used to justify decisions and build trust <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. Adversarial perturbations can alter XAI explanations without changing model predictions, a vulnerability demonstrated in medical imaging and other domains  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. Small perturbations to input can lead to substantial changes in explanations even when model prediction does not change  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. FASS enforces prediction invariance as a precondition for stability measurement, highlighting the importance of this condition  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. The mechanism: The instability of post-hoc explanation methods under adversarial perturbations is a mechanistic consequence of their reliance on local gradients or surrogate models, which are sensitive to small input changes <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. The lack of standardized evaluation metrics for XAI robustness is a critical gap, as most applications rely on anecdotal evidence or expert opinion  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7" data-evidence="E7">7</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. Most evaluations rely on anecdotal evidence or expert opinion rather than robust metrics  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>. Current prevalent methods such as SHAP and LIME provide limited interpretability  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7" data-evidence="E7">7</a></sup>. Lack of standardization across studies, limited dataset availability, and difficulties in generalizing findings  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. The mechanism: Without standardized metrics, it is impossible to compare XAI methods across studies or assess their reliability in practice, leading to inconsistent adoption and potential misuse <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. The instability of XAI methods under adversarial perturbations is not limited to a single domain but manifests across text classification, cybersecurity, and remote sensing  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-18" data-evidence="E18">18</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-39" data-evidence="E39">39</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-13" data-evidence="E13">13</a></sup>. Adversarial perturbations alter feature importance, diminishing the influence of spam-indicative tokens in SMS spam detection  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-18" data-evidence="E18">18</a></sup>. Empirical analysis of adversarial robustness and explainability drift in cybersecurity classifiers shows similar vulnerabilities  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-39" data-evidence="E39">39</a></sup>. XAI-guided adversarial training in remote sensing shows promise but is still nascent  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-13" data-evidence="E13">13</a></sup>. The mechanism: The cross-domain prevalence of XAI instability suggests a common underlying mechanism: post-hoc explanation methods are inherently sensitive to input perturbations, regardless of the data modality <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. As XAI adoption grows in regulated sectors, the gap between explanation stability and model robustness will become a critical failure point, driving demand for standardized robustness metrics  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-23" data-evidence="E23">23</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. The systematic review underscores the urgent need for standardized frameworks to ensure the reliability and effectiveness of XAI applications  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>. Explainability is a key criterion for reinforcing confidence in AI-based medical devices  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup>. FASS is the first benchmark to jointly enforce prediction invariance as a precondition for stability measurement  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. The mechanism: Regulatory bodies and professional standards will increasingly require evidence of XAI robustness before approving AI systems for high-stakes use, creating a market for standardized evaluation tools <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup>. Without standardized robustness metrics, the use of XAI in high-stakes domains like healthcare will pose significant risks, potentially leading to misdiagnosis and eroded trust  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. Adversarial perturbations can manipulate explanations without changing predicted label, misleading auditors and clinicians  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. Explainability is a key criterion for reinforcing confidence in AI-based medical devices  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3" data-evidence="E3">3</a></sup>. Standardized methodologies, larger datasets, and improved generalization are essential for clinical adoption of XAI in Alzheimer&#039;s diagnosis  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. The mechanism: If explanations can be silently corrupted, clinicians and auditors may make incorrect decisions based on misleading information, undermining the very purpose of XAI <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>.</p></div>
<div class="prose-block"><p>If the causal chain holds, the next three years will see a convergence of pressures on XAI robustness <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-23" data-evidence="E23">23</a></sup>. Regulatory bodies, particularly in healthcare and finance, will begin to mandate robustness testing for AI systems that use post-hoc explanations <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-23" data-evidence="E23">23</a></sup>. This will drive the development and adoption of standardized benchmarks like FASS  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup> and robust metrics like RoSHAP <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-36" data-evidence="E36">36</a></sup>. We project that by 2028, at least two major regulatory frameworks (e <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>.g., EU AI Act amendments, FDA guidance) will include explicit requirements for explanation stability under adversarial perturbations <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup>. This will create a market for XAI robustness auditing services, with specialized firms offering certification. In parallel, research will shift from proposing new explanation methods to systematically evaluating their robustness, with a focus on developing inherently stable methods <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. The adoption of vision transformers (ViTs) in medical imaging may accelerate, given their demonstrated higher adversarial robustness compared to CNNs  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup>, but this will not solve the explanation instability problem, as ViTs still rely on post-hoc methods <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. The most significant structural change will be the emergence of &#039;robustness-first&#039; XAI design, where explanation methods are developed with adversarial robustness as a primary objective, not an afterthought <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-34" data-evidence="E34">34</a></sup>. This will be accompanied by a growing body of empirical evidence on the failure modes of current methods, leading to a more cautious approach to XAI deployment in critical decisions <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. This trajectory assumes that (1) XAI adoption continues to grow in regulated sectors, driven by regulatory pressure and market demand for transparency; (2) the research community and industry invest in developing standardized robustness metrics and benchmarks; (3) regulators have the technical capacity to enforce such standards; and (4) no fundamental breakthrough in XAI robustness occurs that would render current concerns obsolete [E2]. If any of these assumptions fail, the trajectory could shift: for example, if regulators adopt a laissez-faire approach, the risks may materialize without mitigation, leading to high-profile failures and subsequent reactive regulation [E2].</p></div>
<div class="prose-block"><p>XAI methods will become inherently robust through architectural innovations, such as the adoption of vision transformers (ViTs) that are more robust to adversarial attacks <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. — While ViTs show higher adversarial robustness than CNNs in computational pathology  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup>, this does not address the instability of post-hoc explanation methods. ViTs still rely on the same explanation techniques (e.g., Grad-CAM, SHAP) that are vulnerable to perturbations. The robustness of the model does not guarantee the robustness of its explanations. — If ViTs become the default architecture, there is a risk that practitioners will assume explanations are also robust, leading to complacency and increased vulnerability <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-23" data-evidence="E23">23</a></sup>. Adversarial training and XAI-guided defenses will become standard practice, mitigating the risks of explanation instability <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-47" data-evidence="E47">47</a></sup>. — Adversarial training is not a universal mitigation, as shown in computational pathology  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup>. XAI-guided adversarial training, while promising in remote sensing  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-13" data-evidence="E13">13</a></sup>, depends on the quality of explanations, which are themselves vulnerable <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup>. The annotation costs and complexity of such approaches limit their scalability  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup>. — If defenses are adopted without rigorous evaluation, they may create a false sense of security, leaving systems vulnerable to novel attacks <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. The lack of standardized metrics will not impede XAI adoption because practitioners will rely on domain expertise and qualitative assessments <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>. — The evidence shows that most XAI evaluations already rely on anecdotal evidence or expert opinion  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>, and this has not prevented adoption. However, this approach is unsustainable in high-stakes domains where errors have severe consequences <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-25" data-evidence="E25">25</a></sup>. The introduction of benchmarks like FASS <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup> indicates a growing recognition of the need for quantitative evaluation <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. — If standardization is delayed, the field may experience a &#039;wild west&#039; period where unreliable XAI methods are deployed, leading to public distrust and eventual regulatory backlash <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>.</p></div>
<div class="prose-block"><p>Adoption of FASS or similar benchmarks by major AI labs or regulatory bodies Publication of large-scale empirical studies on XAI robustness across multiple domains Regulatory guidance from FDA, EMA, or EU AI Act that explicitly mentions explanation stability Development of robust attribution metrics like RoSHAP and their integration into standard toolkits High-profile incidents where manipulated explanations led to incorrect decisions in healthcare or finance The position would be reversed if a large-scale, multi-domain study demonstrated that current XAI methods are robust to adversarial perturbations under realistic conditions, or if a new class of explanation methods emerged that is provably stable <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup>. Additionally, if regulatory bodies explicitly stated that explanation stability is not a concern for AI deployment, the urgency of the issue would diminish <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-23" data-evidence="E23">23</a></sup>. However, given the current evidence, such developments are unlikely in the near term <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. This projection should be revisited in 24 months (mid-2028) to assess whether standardized robustness metrics have been adopted and whether regulatory frameworks have incorporated explanation stability requirements <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. If no progress is made by then, the risks will likely materialize, and the analysis should be updated with new evidence <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>.</p></div>
<div class="prose-block"><p>Link 2: The instability of XAI under adversarial perturbations is directly supported by multiple studies  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6" data-evidence="E6">6</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. Link 3: The lack of standardized evaluation metrics is a well-documented gap across systematic reviews  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7" data-evidence="E7">7</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. Link 4: Cross-domain evidence from text classification <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-18" data-evidence="E18">18</a></sup>, cybersecurity <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-39" data-evidence="E39">39</a></sup>, and remote sensing <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-13" data-evidence="E13">13</a></sup> strengthens the generalizability of the mechanism. Link 5: The projection that regulatory bodies will mandate robustness standards is an extrapolation based on current trends, but it is not directly supported by any source <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6" data-evidence="E6">6</a></sup>. The pace of regulatory change is uncertain and could be slower than anticipated <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>. Quantitative data on the real-world impact of explanation instability in deployed systems is lacking <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>. Most studies focus on specific domains (e.g., medical imaging, text), limiting generalizability to other high-stakes areas like law or finance <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup>. There is no consensus on what constitutes a &#039;robust&#039; explanation, and existing metrics like RoSHAP  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-36" data-evidence="E36">36</a></sup> are not yet widely adopted. The evidence base includes academic journals (e.g., Machine Learning <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup>, Nature Communications <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup>), conference proceedings (e.g., CVPR <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6" data-evidence="E6">6</a></sup>), preprints (arXiv <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-34" data-evidence="E34">34</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-35" data-evidence="E35">35</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-36" data-evidence="E36">36</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>), and systematic reviews (e.g., <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>). This diversity provides cross-type corroboration: academic studies  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4" data-evidence="E4">4</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup> confirm the instability of XAI, while preprints <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup> introduce new benchmarks. However, there is a notable absence of intelligence or NGO sources, which could provide ground-truth on real-world adversarial attacks <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-17" data-evidence="E17">17</a></sup>. The institutional bias of AI labs may overstate capabilities, but the sources here are primarily academic, which mitigates this risk <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. The convergence of findings across source types strengthens the core claim <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4" data-evidence="E4">4</a></sup>.</p></div>
<div class="prose-block"><p>Post-hoc XAI methods, including SHAP and LIME, are unstable under adversarial perturbations, with small input changes leading to substantial changes in explanations even when predictions remain unchanged  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-16" data-evidence="E16">16</a></sup>. The lack of standardized evaluation metrics for XAI robustness is a critical gap, as most applications rely on anecdotal evidence or expert opinion rather than robust metrics  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1" data-evidence="E1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7" data-evidence="E7">7</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9" data-evidence="E9">9</a></sup>. Adversarial perturbations can alter feature importance in text classifiers, diminishing the influence of spam-indicative tokens and degrading interpretability  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-18" data-evidence="E18">18</a></sup>. Vision transformers (ViTs) exhibit higher adversarial robustness than CNNs in computational pathology, but this does not extend to the stability of post-hoc explanations  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-11" data-evidence="E11">11</a></sup>. Novel benchmarks like FASS and metrics like RoSHAP are emerging to address the stability gap, but their adoption is not yet widespread  <sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2" data-evidence="E2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-36" data-evidence="E36">36</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-37" data-evidence="E37">37</a></sup>.</p></div>

